    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d493">

                <pb facs="0489" n="477" sameAs="frus:frus1939v01/pg477"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">740.0011 European War 1939/1365: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in the Soviet Union</hi> (<hi
                        rendition="#italic">Steinhardt</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the
                        Secretary of State </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Moscow</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1939-12-30T15:00:00+03:00">December 30,
                            1939—3 p.m.</date>
                        <lb/> [Received December 30—10:35 a.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>1159. Counselor of the French Embassy told me yesterday in strict confidence that
                    he and his Ambassador had come to the conclusion that the German Government is
                    desirous of seeing a rupture of diplomatic relations between France, Great
                    Britain and the United States and the Soviet Union<anchor n="20"
                        xml:id="d493-f1"/> because of the obvious advantages to Germany which would
                    result therefrom.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Steinhardt</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d493-f1" n="20" xml:id="d493-f1-content">
                <p>Presumably over the question of the Soviet Union’s attack on Finland. See <ref
                        target="#pg952">pp. 952</ref> ff.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
